editorialMd:
Build positions

`drawers` contains two rows: a sorted number list, then a list of queries. Each query is a number to look up. For each query, find the first position in the sorted list whose number is at least the query. Positions, also called indexes, start at 0. If no number is large enough, record the length of the sorted list. Put the answers in query order.

Return the answer

Complete `insertionTabsProofStrip` so it returns `positions`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `positions` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(q log(n+1)); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the length of the sorted number list; q is the number of queries. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function insertionTabsProofStrip(drawers) {
  const a=drawers; let out=[];
  for(const x of a[1]){let l=0,r=a[0].length;while(l<r){const m=Math.floor((l+r)/2);if(a[0][m]<x)l=m+1;else r=m;}out.push(l);}
  return out;
}


